  "answer": "### Issue Presented\nWhat is the deadline for filing an estate tax return, and is it the same as the individual income tax deadline?\n\n### Short Answer\n\n### Accounting Treatment\nFor accounting purposes, the estate tax return must be prepared based on the fair market value of the decedent's assets at the time of death. The estate must account for all assets, liabilities, and the resulting net estate to determine the taxable estate.\n\n### Tax Treatment\n\n### Differences and Reconciliation\nThe key difference lies in the filing deadlines: estate tax returns are due within six months of death, while individual income tax returns are due annually on April 15. This distinction is crucial for compliance and planning purposes, as the estate tax is a one-time tax based on the decedent's estate, whereas individual income tax is an ongoing annual obligation.\n\n### Practical Note\nTaxpayers should ensure timely filing of the estate tax return to avoid penalties. It is advisable to consult with a tax professional to navigate the complexities of estate valuation and tax obligations, especially if the estate includes various types of assets or liabilities.",
